---
import PhotoPlaceholder from '../PhotoPlaceholder.astro';
import VisionFigure from '../VisionFigure.astro';
import SectionHead from '../SectionHead.astro';
---

<section data-vision-section class="band-ink section">
  <SectionHead num="4 / 14 — How we get around" heading="Vegas as a node, not an island." />

  <div class="container-page">
    <PhotoPlaceholder
      variant="ink"
      description="Brightline West rendering, or a regional rail station with a train arriving — the Strip skyline in the background, the Spring Mountains beyond."
    />
  </div>

  <div class="container-narrow">
    <div class="vision-prose">
      <p>
        The first plausible regional-rail connection out of Las&nbsp;Vegas in any of our lifetimes —
        Brightline West to Southern California — breaks ground this decade and is scheduled to open
        in 2029. From a station near the I-15 corridor, a passenger should be able to reach the
        Strip, downtown, or the airport without renting a car. That last part is the political
        fight: the airport-to-Strip rail link the casino and taxi industries killed in the 1990s
        would need to finally get built, against essentially the same opposition that killed it the
        first time.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="container-page">
    <VisionFigure num="4" title="Vegas as a node, not an island">
      <svg
        class="region-map"
        viewBox="0 0 600 360"
        xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
        role="img"
        aria-label="Map of the Western US rail network with Las Vegas as a hub. A solid line connects Las Vegas to Los Angeles (under construction). Dashed lines run to Reno, Salt Lake, and Phoenix (future)."
      >
        <text class="region-axis" x="300" y="30" text-anchor="middle">The Western network</text>
        <path class="region-line-built" d="M180 200 Q230 230, 280 250"></path>
        <path class="region-line-future" d="M180 200 Q140 140, 100 90"></path>
        <path class="region-line-future" d="M180 200 Q260 130, 380 90"></path>
        <path class="region-line-future" d="M180 200 Q300 240, 420 280"></path>
        <circle class="region-dot-vegas" cx="180" cy="200" r="9"></circle>
        <text class="region-label-vegas" x="180" y="180" text-anchor="middle">Las&nbsp;Vegas</text>
        <circle class="region-dot" cx="280" cy="250" r="6"></circle>
        <text class="region-label" x="280" y="275" text-anchor="middle">Los Angeles</text>
        <circle class="region-dot" cx="100" cy="90" r="6"></circle>
        <text class="region-label" x="100" y="80" text-anchor="middle">Reno</text>
        <circle class="region-dot" cx="380" cy="90" r="6"></circle>
        <text class="region-label" x="380" y="80" text-anchor="middle">Salt Lake</text>
        <circle class="region-dot" cx="420" cy="280" r="6"></circle>
        <text class="region-label" x="420" y="305" text-anchor="middle">Phoenix</text>
        <text class="region-legend" x="60" y="340">─── built / under construction</text>
        <text class="region-legend" x="320" y="340">─ ─ future · the next decade-plus</text>
      </svg>
      <Fragment slot="caption">
        Brightline West breaks ground this decade. Phoenix, Salt Lake, and Reno are slower work —
        but a metro of 2.3 million should be planning for them, the same way California planned for
        fifty years of Pacific corridor rail before it started laying track.
      </Fragment>
    </VisionFigure>
  </div>

  <div class="container-narrow">
    <div class="vision-prose">
      <p>
        Look further out and a real Western network appears. Phoenix and Las&nbsp;Vegas should be
        connected by passenger rail; both are growing past the carrying capacity of I-93 and I-10.
        Salt Lake to Las&nbsp;Vegas is on the same map. Reno over the Sierra is harder but not
        impossible. None of these will get built in the next five years. Some of them might within
        twenty.
      </p>
      <p>
        The point of including them in a vision document is not to promise them. It's to insist that
        they belong in the picture, alongside the BRT we're already paying for and the highway
        expansions we keep paying for. A metro of 2.3 million is not an island. We should stop
        building like one.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .region-map {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    max-height: 360px;
  }
  .region-map .region-dot {
    fill: currentColor;
  }
  .region-map .region-dot-vegas {
    fill: var(--color-accent);
  }
  .region-map .region-label {
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 700;
    fill: currentColor;
  }
  .region-map .region-label-vegas {
    fill: var(--color-accent);
    font-weight: 900;
  }
  .region-map .region-line-built {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--color-accent);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }
  .region-map .region-line-future {
    fill: none;
    stroke: currentColor;
    stroke-width: 1.5;
    stroke-dasharray: 3 4;
    opacity: 0.45;
  }
  .region-map .region-axis,
  .region-map .region-legend {
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    font-weight: 600;
    fill: var(--vp-text-muted);
  }
</style>
